  <h2>Why Is the Same MRI So Expensive at the Hospital?</h2>

  <p>
    Most people assume that higher cost = higher quality. But when it comes to
    medical imaging, that logic fails. Hospitals and outpatient imaging centers
    often use the <strong>exact same machines</strong> and <strong
      >board-certified radiologists</strong
    >.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Facility Fees</h3>
  <p>
    Hospitals add a <strong>“facility fee”</strong>—a charge for being seen at
    their location. This alone can add <strong>hundreds to over $1,000</strong> to
    your bill.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Insurance-Driven Price Inflation</h3>
  <p>
    Hospitals negotiate higher prices with insurers to maximize reimbursement.
    But <strong>uninsured patients get stuck</strong> with these inflated list prices—called
    “chargemaster” rates.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>💡 A 2023 study by Health Care Cost Institute</strong> found that the
      <strong>average hospital MRI is over $1,100</strong>, compared to <strong
        >under $500</strong
      > at independent centers.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h3>3. Unbundled Billing</h3>
